      "context": "yms in court records pertaining to this case and (2) seal a court record (Disclosure Document) listing the Does’ actual names. As a result, the Does have never been publicly identified, by name, as the plaintiffs in this case. For the reasons explained below, we affirm the Court of Appeals in part and hold that the order sealing the Disclosure Document was an abuse of discretion because the trial court’s findings are not sufficient to satisfy GR 15 or Seattle Times Co. v. Ishikawa, 97 Wn.2d 30, 640 P.2d 716 (1982). For similar reasons, the trial court abused its discretion in allowing the Does to remain in pseudonym, and we hold the pseudonym issue is not moot. Given our resolution of these issues, we decline to reach the evidentiary challenges raised in Zink’s cross petition for 1 Public Records Act, ch. 42.56 RCW. 2 John Doe P et al. v. Thurston County et al., No. 102976-4 review. We remand to the trial court with instructions to (1) unseal the Disclosure Document, (2) use the Does’ actual names",
